WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:03.374
<v Susanne>Same tomato, two knives.

2
00:00:03.374 --> 00:00:06.440
<v Susanne>One crushes – the other slices.

3
00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:11.720
<v Susanne>Today: which knives you really need, what they should cost

4
00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:13.880
<v Susanne>– and how to keep them sharp.

5
00:00:15.688 --> 00:00:17.135
<v Susanne>Hi, I'm Susanne.

6
00:00:17.135 --> 00:00:23.196
<v Susanne>Peter's off today – next time he'll show you how to cut like a pro.

7
00:00:23.196 --> 00:00:26.000
<v Susanne>Today it's all about the tools.

8
00:00:26.349 --> 00:00:28.397
<v Susanne>What makes a knife good?

9
00:00:28.397 --> 00:00:35.308
<v Susanne>First, the steel: hard, stainless knife steel – fifty-six to fifty-eight Rockwell

10
00:00:35.308 --> 00:00:40.000
<v Susanne>– stays sharp for weeks and is easy to sharpen at home.

11
00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:44.993
<v Susanne>Look for a blade that runs right through the handle – the full tang

12
00:00:44.993 --> 00:00:48.426
<v Susanne>– and a handle that fits your hand, with no gaps.

13
00:00:48.426 --> 00:00:49.686
<v Susanne>Forged or stamped?

14
00:00:49.686 --> 00:00:54.940
<v Susanne>Forged is heavier and stiffer, but a good stamped knife works just as well.

15
00:00:59.229 --> 00:01:03.816
<v Susanne>Japanese steels are harder – sixty Rockwell and more.

16
00:01:03.816 --> 00:01:11.000
<v Susanne>They stay sharp longer, but they're brittle: no bones, no frozen food, no twisting.

17
00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:16.454
<v Susanne>Carbon steel is the sharpest of all – but it rusts.

18
00:01:16.454 --> 00:01:22.000
<v Susanne>And ceramic stays sharp for ages – until it hits the floor.

19
00:01:22.772 --> 00:01:25.054
<v Susanne>So how much should you spend?

20
00:01:25.054 --> 00:01:31.269
<v Susanne>Forget the two-euro knife from the supermarket: soft steel, dull within a week,

21
00:01:31.269 --> 00:01:33.000
<v Susanne>impossible to sharpen.

22
00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:36.737
<v Susanne>A good chef's knife starts at about forty euros.

23
00:01:36.737 --> 00:01:40.422
<v Susanne>Between sixty and a hundred you get a knife for life.

24
00:01:40.422 --> 00:01:45.080
<v Susanne>Above a hundred and fifty you pay for beauty – not for better cuts.

25
00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:50.815
<v Susanne>You don't need a block full of knives.

26
00:01:50.815 --> 00:01:55.581
<v Susanne>Three do ninety percent of the work: a chef's knife, twenty centimetres,

27
00:01:55.581 --> 00:01:57.170
<v Susanne>for meat and vegetables.

28
00:01:57.170 --> 00:02:00.149
<v Susanne>A small utility knife, about ten centimetres.

29
00:02:00.149 --> 00:02:03.260
<v Susanne>And a peeling knife with a short, curved blade.

30
00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:12.982
<v Susanne>The chef's knife is your workhorse: it chops, slices and dices

31
00:02:12.982 --> 00:02:18.000
<v Susanne>– and if you cook a lot, spend the most on this one.

32
00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:24.580
<v Susanne>The small knife is for everything fiddly: garlic, mushrooms, strawberries, a lemon.

33
00:02:26.543 --> 00:02:32.361
<v Susanne>And if you love crusty bread: a bread knife with a serrated edge.

34
00:02:32.361 --> 00:02:38.000
<v Susanne>It's the one knife that stays sharp for years without any care.

35
00:02:38.568 --> 00:02:40.926
<v Susanne>Now the edge – the grind.

36
00:02:40.926 --> 00:02:48.000
<v Susanne>Almost every kitchen knife has a plain edge, ground on both sides into a V.

37
00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:52.977
<v Susanne>European knives are ground at fifteen to twenty degrees per side

38
00:02:52.977 --> 00:02:54.657
<v Susanne>– robust and forgiving.

39
00:02:54.657 --> 00:02:59.261
<v Susanne>Japanese knives at ten to fifteen – sharper, but more delicate.

40
00:02:59.261 --> 00:03:01.891
<v Susanne>A serrated edge saws through crusts.

41
00:03:01.891 --> 00:03:04.010
<v Susanne>And the hollows on a santoku?

42
00:03:04.010 --> 00:03:06.860
<v Susanne>They stop slices sticking to the blade.

43
00:03:08.393 --> 00:03:14.122
<v Susanne>Single-bevel blades, ground on one side only, are for specialists – sushi,

44
00:03:14.122 --> 00:03:14.897
<v Susanne>filleting.

45
00:03:14.897 --> 00:03:18.226
<v Susanne>For home cooking: double-bevel, plain edge.

46
00:03:18.226 --> 00:03:19.000
<v Susanne>That's it.

47
00:03:19.409 --> 00:03:25.490
<v Susanne>A knife is only as good as its edge – so let's talk sharpening.

48
00:03:25.490 --> 00:03:28.000
<v Susanne>Two tools, and you're set.

49
00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:30.464
<v Susanne>First, the honing steel.

50
00:03:30.464 --> 00:03:33.983
<v Susanne>It doesn't grind – it straightens the edge.

51
00:03:33.983 --> 00:03:39.630
<v Susanne>Before cooking, five or six light strokes per side at twenty degrees.

52
00:03:39.630 --> 00:03:41.267
<v Susanne>Hard Japanese steel?

53
00:03:41.267 --> 00:03:42.740
<v Susanne>Use a ceramic rod.

54
00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:45.292
<v Susanne>Second, the whetstone.

55
00:03:45.292 --> 00:03:47.248
<v Susanne>Soak it for ten minutes.

56
00:03:47.248 --> 00:03:52.380
<v Susanne>Grit one thousand does the sharpening, three thousand polishes.

57
00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:59.518
<v Susanne>Keep the angle steady – a clip-on guide helps – and push the edge across the stone,

58
00:03:59.518 --> 00:04:00.387
<v Susanne>heel to tip.

59
00:04:00.387 --> 00:04:02.780
<v Susanne>Every couple of months is plenty.

60
00:04:06.797 --> 00:04:08.577
<v Susanne>Pull-through sharpeners?

61
00:04:08.577 --> 00:04:11.173
<v Susanne>Quick, but coarse – they eat steel.

62
00:04:11.173 --> 00:04:16.514
<v Susanne>A rolling sharpener with a magnetic angle guide is the gentler shortcut.

63
00:04:16.514 --> 00:04:17.997
<v Susanne>Serrated or ceramic?

64
00:04:17.997 --> 00:04:20.000
<v Susanne>Those go to a professional.

65
00:04:20.500 --> 00:04:22.827
<v Susanne>The test: a ripe tomato.

66
00:04:22.827 --> 00:04:23.603
<v Susanne>Sharp?

67
00:04:23.603 --> 00:04:26.900
<v Susanne>It slides in under its own weight.

68
00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:31.608
<v Susanne>The board matters as much as the knife.

69
00:04:31.608 --> 00:04:36.104
<v Susanne>Wood is best – end-grain is the gentlest on the edge.

70
00:04:36.104 --> 00:04:39.921
<v Susanne>Plastic is fine and can go in the dishwasher.

71
00:04:39.921 --> 00:04:42.466
<v Susanne>Bamboo is harder on the blade.

72
00:04:42.466 --> 00:04:44.841
<v Susanne>And glass, stone or ceramic?

73
00:04:44.841 --> 00:04:45.520
<v Susanne>Never.

74
00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:47.980
<v Susanne>They kill an edge in minutes.

75
00:04:49.725 --> 00:04:55.623
<v Susanne>Buy one big board – forty by thirty centimetres at least, and thick,

76
00:04:55.623 --> 00:04:57.357
<v Susanne>so it doesn't slide.

77
00:04:57.357 --> 00:05:01.000
<v Susanne>A damp cloth underneath keeps it in place.

78
00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:07.462
<v Susanne>And one rule for hygiene: raw meat, poultry and fish get their own board

79
00:05:07.462 --> 00:05:11.590
<v Susanne>– a plastic one you can wash at sixty degrees.

80
00:05:11.590 --> 00:05:15.000
<v Susanne>Vegetables and bread stay on the wood.

81
00:05:15.500 --> 00:05:18.685
<v Susanne>Wash wood by hand, dry it standing up,

82
00:05:18.685 --> 00:05:22.959
<v Susanne>and oil it once a month with food-safe mineral oil.

83
00:05:22.959 --> 00:05:24.049
<v Susanne>Deep grooves?

84
00:05:24.049 --> 00:05:26.060
<v Susanne>Sand it – or replace it.

85
00:05:27.500 --> 00:05:29.918
<v Susanne>Knives never go in the dishwasher.

86
00:05:29.918 --> 00:05:31.980
<v Susanne>Wash by hand, dry right away.

87
00:05:35.500 --> 00:05:40.288
<v Susanne>Store them on a magnetic strip or in a block – never loose in a drawer.

88
00:05:40.288 --> 00:05:43.660
<v Susanne>And scrape the board with the spine, not the edge.

89
00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:54.561
<v Susanne>So: three knives – chef's, utility, peeling – and a bread knife for crust.

90
00:05:54.561 --> 00:05:59.389
<v Susanne>Stainless steel, forty to a hundred euros for the big one.

91
00:05:59.389 --> 00:06:02.803
<v Susanne>A honing steel and a thousand-grit stone.

92
00:06:02.803 --> 00:06:06.715
<v Susanne>A big wooden board, a plastic one for raw meat.

93
00:06:06.715 --> 00:06:08.880
<v Susanne>That's a kitchen for life.

94
00:06:10.405 --> 00:06:17.759
<v Susanne>Next time, Peter shows you how to use them – from julienne to a perfect dice.

95
00:06:17.759 --> 00:06:19.000
<v Susanne>See you then!
